    "narrative": "This is a textbook **x86-64 Linux shellcode** designed to spawn an interactive shell via `execve('/bin/sh', NULL, NULL)`. The code exhibits three classic shellcode construction techniques simultaneously: **XOR zeroing** to avoid null bytes, **MOVABS** to embed the path string as a 64-bit immediate, and **MOV AL** (one-byte load) to set the syscall number without null bytes in the encoding.\n\nThe null-byte avoidance is critical for exploitation contexts: shellcode is often injected through string-handling vulnerabilities (buffer overflows via `strcpy`, `gets`). These functions terminate on null bytes (0x00), so shellcode must not contain them. Every technique in this sample is specifically chosen to produce null-free bytecode.\n\nThe 25-instruction sequence is self-contained and position-independent — it can execute correctly from any memory address without relocation. This is the signature of shellcode intended for injection into an exploited process, not a legitimately compiled program.",
